00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:16.900
The move raises questions under federal impact assessment and marine protection rules.

00:01:17.540 --> 00:01:22.280
Terminal development would involve marine components subject to existing federal oversight.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:28.060
Routing through the Salish Sea introduces additional considerations for vessel traffic and habitat.

00:01:28.580 --> 00:01:32.540
Project teams should map federal triggers against the accelerated schedule.

00:01:33.340 --> 00:01:38.160
When federal fast-track mechanisms apply to pipeline terminals, review fisheries act

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:43.720
and impact assessment act overlap early. Confirm whether marine terminal components trigger

00:01:43.720 --> 00:01:49.000
additional habitat or spill response planning. Cross-check provincial permitting requirements

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:53.200
in British Columbia against the federal timeline to avoid sequential delays.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:02:00.440
Habitat provisions under federal legislation often require separate evaluation even under accelerated schedules.

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:08.460
A client submits a pipeline terminal proposal and asks why federal fast track language does not automatically waive provincial reviews.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:13.980
Federal and provincial regimes operate on separate triggers even when timelines are compressed.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:20.040
field experience shows that marine terminal footprints often engage fisheries act habitat provisions

00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:25.640
before the impact assessment act process finishes its scoping. The detail that separates

00:02:25.640 --> 00:02:31.380
experience teams is recognizing that Salish Sea Routing adds vessel traffic modeling requirements

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:37.100
that standard terrestrial pipeline assessments omit. The most common mistake is assuming

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:41.360
the fast track notice replaces the need for a standalone marine risk assessment.

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:45.760
The fix is to run a Parallel Fisheries Act pre-application meeting.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.520
In the same week, the federal notice is published.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:53.900
Early coordination prevents later conflicts between compressed federal schedules and provincial

00:02:53.900 --> 00:02:54.720
review cycles.

